<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Euzkadi (1936-1937)</h1><p class="st-meta">1936 CE – 1937 CE · capital Bilbao</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Basque autonomous government, formed during the Spanish Civil War and defeated within a year, after the bombing of Guernica.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> granted the Basque Country a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-autonomy-of-the-basque-country-1936">Statute of Autonomy</a> in October 1936. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-aguirre">José Antonio Aguirre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basque-nationalist-party">Basque Nationalist Party</a> was sworn in as first lehendakari under the oak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> on 7 October 1936.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government controlled only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a>. It raised its own army, issued money and passports, and protected the Catholic Church, unlike much of Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao's</a> industry and port were its base, defended by the fortified Iron Ring.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Franco's Army of the North attacked in 1937. German and Italian aircraft destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> on 26 April 1937, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> fell on 19 June. Basque troops surrendered to the Italians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santona">Santoña</a> in August. The government went into exile, and Basque autonomy was restored only in 1979.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/euzkadi-1936-1937.jpg" alt="Euzkadi (1936-1937)" width="320" height="179"><figcaption>Image: Daniele Schirmo aka Frankie688 · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Basque_Country.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1936 CE – 1937 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bilbao</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Antonio Aguirre</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Basque, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Basque Autonomous Government, Basque Country (1936-1937)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=euzkadi-1936-1937" aria-label="Euzkadi (1936-1937) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4614 651 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Euzkadi (1936-1937) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4934 907L4909 989M4934 907L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=euzkadi-1936-1937">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

